    <h2>The 5 Real Causes of Hair Loss, and How to Tell Them Apart</h2>
    <p>Most people assume all hair loss is the same thing at different speeds. It is not. These five causes behave differently, and each one calls for a different response.</p>

    <div class="vy-cause">
      <div class="vy-cause-photo"><img src="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-383-simply-revival/images/crown-thinning.jpg" alt="Early thinning visible at the crown of a man's head" loading="lazy"></div>
      <div class="vy-cause-body">
        <h3>1. Pattern hair loss <span class="vy-chip">Most common in men</span></h3>
        <p>By far the most common cause, and strongly hereditary, especially through your father's side. It follows a predictable map: the temples and hairline pull back, the crown thins, and the change unfolds slowly over years. Doctors call it androgenetic alopecia. Most men know it as male pattern baldness, and a large majority of men experience some degree of it by later adulthood.</p>
        <p class="vy-sign"><b>Tell-tale signs:</b> family history, thinning concentrated at the temples and crown, gradual timeline.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="vy-cause">
      <div class="vy-cause-photo"><img src="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-383-simply-revival/images/stress-shedding.jpg" alt="Loose hair strands on a white pillowcase" loading="lazy"></div>
      <div class="vy-cause-body">
        <h3>2. Stress shedding <span class="vy-chip vy-gray">Usually temporary</span></h3>
        <p>A physically or emotionally intense event, such as an illness, surgery, or a brutal few months at work, can push a large share of your follicles into their resting phase at once. Two to three months later, hair starts coming out in noticeable amounts all over the head, often in the shower or on the pillow.</p>
        <p class="vy-sign"><b>Tell-tale signs:</b> sudden, diffuse shedding that begins a few months after a stressful event. It typically resolves on its own within months once the trigger passes.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="vy-cause">
      <div class="vy-cause-photo"><img src="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-383-simply-revival/images/nutrient-foods.jpg" alt="Foods rich in iron, zinc, vitamin D, and B vitamins on a kitchen table" loading="lazy"></div>
      <div class="vy-cause-body">
        <h3>3. Nutritional gaps</h3>
        <p>Hair is metabolically expensive, and your body treats it as optional. When iron, zinc, vitamin D, or B vitamins run low, hair is one of the first places it shows. This one is easy to miss because the rest of your diet can look reasonably healthy.</p>
        <p class="vy-sign"><b>Tell-tale signs:</b> all-over thinning, often alongside fatigue or brittle nails. Basic blood work at a doctor's office can confirm or rule it out.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="vy-cause">
      <div class="vy-cause-photo"><img src="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-383-simply-revival/images/scalp-exam.jpg" alt="Doctor examining a patient's scalp during a check-up" loading="lazy"></div>
      <div class="vy-cause-body">
        <h3>4. Thyroid, hormonal, and scalp conditions</h3>
        <p>An underactive or overactive thyroid can disrupt the hair growth cycle across the whole scalp. So can certain hormonal shifts, and scalp conditions such as seborrheic dermatitis or fungal infection, which inflame the skin the hair grows from.</p>
        <p class="vy-sign"><b>Tell-tale signs:</b> thinning that arrives with other symptoms, such as changes in energy, weight, or skin, or an itchy, flaky, irritated scalp. These are worth raising with a doctor.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="vy-cause">
      <div class="vy-cause-photo"><img src="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-383-simply-revival/images/traction-bun.jpg" alt="Man with hair pulled tightly into a bun, showing tension at the hairline" loading="lazy"></div>
      <div class="vy-cause-body">
        <h3>5. Traction alopecia</h3>
        <p>Purely mechanical. Years of tight styles, harsh brushing, or constant friction can damage follicles along the exact lines where the tension sits. It has nothing to do with hormones or genetics, and catching it early matters, because prolonged traction can become permanent.</p>
        <p class="vy-sign"><b>Tell-tale signs:</b> thinning that traces precisely where the pulling happens, most often along the edges and hairline.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:26px"><b style="color:var(--ink)">Why this matters:</b> a fix aimed at the wrong cause does nothing. Iron will not restore a genetically receding hairline, and no hormone-focused approach will help thinning caused by a thyroid problem. Match the response to the cause, or you are burning money and months.</p>

    <div class="vy-section-rule"></div>

    <h2 id="genetic">If Your Answers Point to Genetic Hair Loss, Here Is What Is Actually Happening</h2>
    <p>Genetic hair loss is not about poor circulation, a dirty scalp, or wearing hats. It comes down to a hormone called <b style="color:var(--ink)">DHT</b>, short for dihydrotestosterone, which your body makes naturally from testosterone.</p>
    <p>If you inherited follicles that are sensitive to DHT, the hormone gradually miniaturizes them. Each new hair grows in slightly thinner and shorter than the one before it, until some follicles stop producing visible hair at all. That is why pattern hair loss is gradual, and why it concentrates at the temples and crown, where follicles tend to be most DHT-sensitive.</p>

    <div class="vy-dht-steps">
      <div class="vy-dht-step">
        <div class="vy-stage">Stage 1</div>
        <svg width="34" height="44" viewBox="0 0 34 44" fill="none"><path d="M17 42V10" stroke="#2E6D6C" stroke-width="7" stroke-linecap="round"/><ellipse cx="17" cy="38" rx="9" ry="6" fill="#E7F0EF"/><path d="M17 42V10" stroke="#2E6D6C" stroke-width="7" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
        <p><b>Healthy follicle.</b> Hair grows thick, anchored deep, on a full growth cycle.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vy-dht-step">
        <div class="vy-stage">Stage 2</div>
        <svg width="34" height="44" viewBox="0 0 34 44" fill="none"><ellipse cx="17" cy="38" rx="8" ry="5.5" fill="#F6E8CE"/><path d="M17 42V14" stroke="#4C8483" stroke-width="4.5" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
        <p><b>DHT exposure.</b> Sensitive follicles shrink. Each new hair comes in thinner and shorter.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vy-dht-step">
        <div class="vy-stage">Stage 3</div>
        <svg width="34" height="44" viewBox="0 0 34 44" fill="none"><ellipse cx="17" cy="38" rx="7" ry="5" fill="#F3D9D3"/><path d="M17 42V24" stroke="#9BB5B4" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
        <p><b>Miniaturized.</b> The follicle produces only fine, wispy hair, then eventually none.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>Two things follow from that. First, genetic hair loss is progressive: left alone, it continues on its own schedule. Second, because the mechanism is so well understood, it is also the most addressable type. There are established ways to approach it, ranging from prescription medications a doctor can walk you through, to non-prescription approaches aimed at the same DHT pathway.</p>

    <figure class="vy-hero-figure" style="margin:6px 0 28px">
      <img src="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-383-simply-revival/images/doctor-consult.jpg" alt="Man discussing hair loss options with a doctor in a clinic" width="1600" height="1000" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption>Because the DHT mechanism is well mapped, genetic hair loss is one of the few types with established, well-studied ways to address it.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>Prescription routes are one path. If you would rather start with a plant-based, non-prescription way to work on that same DHT pathway, here is a good place to begin.</p>

